---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const stack = ['Python', 'CQC API', 'Companies House API', 'Data matching'];
---

<Base
  title="Lead Enrichment Pipeline Case Study | Ishraq Alam"
  description="A Python pipeline combining the CQC and Companies House APIs to enrich ~180 care companies with decision-maker data."
>
  <article>
    <section class="cs-hero">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <a href="/case-studies/" class="back">← All case studies</a>
        <span class="eyebrow">Case study · In progress</span>
        <h1>Lead Enrichment Pipeline</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          How I approach a messy data problem: ~180 care companies needed decision-maker
          and structure data for targeted outreach, and no clean source existed. So I'm
          building one.
        </p>
        <ul class="chips">
          {stack.map((item) => <li class="chip chip-blue">{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pb-0">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <span class="eyebrow">The problem</span>
        <h2>Two public datasets, zero clean joins</h2>
        <p>
          The CQC knows who provides care and how well. Companies House knows who owns
          and runs those companies. Neither, on its own, tells an SDR who to call.
          Matching the two by hand across ~180 target accounts would take weeks and be
          out of date before it was finished.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pb-0">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <span class="eyebrow">The build</span>
        <h2>Automated extraction and matching</h2>
        <p>
          A Python script pulls provider records from the CQC API, resolves each
          provider to its registered company via the Companies House API, and extracts
          officers and organisational structure. The result: two disconnected public
          datasets turned into a single enriched contact list, refreshable on demand.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="container narrow">
        <span class="eyebrow">Status</span>
        <h2>In progress, and that's the point</h2>
        <p>
          This one is deliberately shown unfinished. It's an example of how I approach
          messy data problems: find the public sources, automate the joins, and hand the
          team something repeatable, not a one-off spreadsheet.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a href="/case-studies/competitive-intel/" class="btn btn-ghost">Next: Competitive Intelligence Matrix →</a>
          <a href="/enterprise/" class="btn btn-primary">Work with me</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

  <style>
    .cs-hero { padding-top: 80px; padding-bottom: 0; }

    .back {
      display: inline-block;
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: var(--text-dim);
      margin-bottom: 24px;
    }

    .pb-0 { padding-bottom: 0; }

    .cta-row {
      display: flex;
      gap: 14px;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      margin-top: 28px;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
